Where to get the match data
You don't need to scrape anything. The cleanest free option for a self-hosted hobby project is openfootball/worldcup.json — free, public-domain football data with no API key required, including the Canada/USA/Mexico 2026 schedule as JSON. You seed your database from it once and you're done for fixtures. GitHub

For live results during the tournament you have two free-tier routes. API-Football covers every 2026 match — schedule, standings, and bracket — available now via league=1 and season=2026, with live data activating as matches play. Or football-data.org includes World Cup fixtures on its free tier, though with more aggressive pagination and without venue metadata or Round-of-32 placeholders. Paid options exist (TheStatsAPI, Sportmonks, Statorium) but you don't need them for a friends-and-family pickems site. API-FootballTheStatsAPI

A nice fallback worth building anyway: an admin screen where you manually enter final scores. It makes the app fully self-contained if an API hiccups, and during a 104-match tournament that's reassuring.
Data model

The 2026 format shapes a couple of design decisions. There are 12 groups of four named A through L, with three points for a win and one for a draw, and 32 teams reach the knockouts — the top two from each group plus the eight best third-placed teams — then it's Round of 32, Round of 16, quarter-finals, semi-finals, and final. The practical consequence: your stage field needs a round_of_32 value that older tournament schemas lack, and knockout matches won't have known teams until the group stage resolves, so team columns on a match must be nullable with a text placeholder ("Winner Group A", "3rd best B/E/F").

A phased build plan

1. Backend skeleton + DB. Get Go talking to Postgres, run your migrations, seed teams and matches from openfootball. No auth yet — just a GET /api/matches that returns the schedule. This teaches you the language's HTTP and DB story end to end.
2. Auth. Register, login, logout with hashed passwords and session cookies. This is the meatiest "learn the language" piece — error handling, middleware, request context.
3. Picks. Authenticated users submit and edit predictions, with server-side kickoff locking and the unique constraint doing the heavy lifting.
4. Scoring + leaderboard. Write the pure scoring function, the recompute-on-finish step, and a GET /api/leagues/:id/standings query that sums points per member.
5. Leagues. Create a league, generate a join code, join via code, scope the leaderboard to members.
6.Angular frontend. Now wire up the UI against an API you trust: fixtures list, pick entry forms, a leaderboard, league management, and an auth guard on the routes.
7. Results sync + admin. The worker that polls the football API and the manual-entry admin fallback.
8. Dockerize and self-host. Compose file with API, Postgres, and Caddy; point a domain at your box.

Create a new monorepo project called worldcup-pickems with two directories: backend (Go module named github.com/ryhiltabrand/worldcup-pickems/backend) and frontend. In the backend, initialize a Go module, create a basic main.go that starts an HTTP server on port 8080 with a health check endpoint at GET /api/health that returns {"status":"ok"}. Also create a docker-compose.yml at the root with services for postgres (postgres:16, with a worldcup_pickems database, user and password from .env), and a stub for the api service. Create a .env.example file with the required variables.